NG_FINDINGS = "NIGERIA (NG) - findings for cl-stack-calendars#45\n\nIssue item 4 (Democracy Day 2018): CONFIRMED. 29 May 2018 was the holiday (Ministry of Interior release of 28 May 2018,\nhttps://fmino.gov.ng/federal-government-declares-tuesday-29th-may-as-public-holiday-to-mark-2018-democracy-day/). The State House\nstatement of 6 June 2018 says June 12 'in future years will replace May 29th'. 12 June 2018 was NOT a public holiday. The\nPublic Holidays (Amendment) Act 2019 was assented on 10 June 2019 (news); 29 May 2019 (still Democracy Day + inauguration) and\n12 June 2019 were BOTH holidays. Starter fix: 05-29 :from 2000 :to 2019 (or 1999 if 29 May 1999 counts - it was handover day,\nno source found), 06-12 :from 2019.\n\nIssue item 5 (Sunday observance, NG 2020-12-28): CONFIRMED as a declared day, but NOT statutory. The Act (Decree 3 of 1979 s.5,\nCap. P40 LFN 2004 s.5) says a Saturday/Sunday holiday gets NO day in lieu. In practice the Minister of Interior declares the next\nweekday(s) every time (seen 2011, 2015-2025, both Saturday and Sunday). The 2020 release: 'Friday 25, Monday 28 December 2020 and\nFriday, January 1, 2021'. So NG needs a SAT_SUN -> next working day rule (pairs push to Mon+Tue), not a Sunday-only rule.\n\nOther statute facts:\n- Boxing Day is NOT in the statutory Schedule (1979 Decree, 1984 amendment, LFN 2004) but is declared every year.\n- The Schedule gives one day per Eid; the Ministry always declares two days for Fitr and Kabir (three for Fitr 2024), one for Maulud.\n- Workers' Day from the 1984 Decree (28 Aug 1984) -> first 1 May 1985.\n- Easter: Good Friday and Easter Monday (Western).\n\nConfidence by year: 2016-2026 primary (Ministry of Interior releases on interior.gov.ng via Wayback, and the same releases reposted on\nfmino.gov.ng, Federal Ministry of Information - fetched directly through its WordPress API), with a few 2017-2019 items only in\nnewspapers (marked SECONDARY in the doc title). 2015 secondary (newspapers quoting the Ministry). 2011-2014 secondary and PARTIAL\n(fixed days + one or two declarations each); 2010 likewise (Yar'Adua mourning 05-06, Democracy Day in lieu 05-31). 2000-2009 and 2027: no official declaration found -> omitted from NG_YEARS\n(python-holidays/timeanddate only). 2026: Christmas/Boxing 2026 not yet declared (expected Monday 2026-12-28 in lieu of Saturday 26th).\n\npython-holidays 0.109 disagreements (declaration wins):\n- 2012 Kabir: declared Thu 10-25 + Fri 10-26; p-h 10-26, 10-27, 10-29.\n- 2017 Kabir: declared 09-01, 09-04; p-h 09-01..09-05. 2017 Fitr: declared 06-26, 06-27 (Eid itself Sunday 06-25).\n- 2018 Kabir: declared 08-21, 08-22; p-h 08-22, 08-23.\n- 2020 Fitr: declared 05-25, 05-26; p-h 05-24, 05-25, 05-26. 2020 Kabir: declared 07-30, 07-31; p-h 07-31, 08-01, 08-03.\n- 2021 Fitr: declared 05-12, 05-13; p-h 05-13, 05-14.\n- 2022: declared 05-02, 05-03 only (p-h adds 05-04); Kabir declared 07-11, 07-12 (p-h 07-09..07-12).\n- 2024 Fitr: declared 04-09, 04-10, 04-11; p-h 04-10, 04-11. 2024 Kabir: declared 06-17, 06-18; p-h 06-16..06-18.\n- 2025 Fitr: declared 03-31, 04-01; p-h 03-30..04-01. 2025 Kabir: declared 06-06, 06-09; p-h 06-06, 06-07, 06-09.\n- 2026 Kabir: declared 05-27, 05-28 (p-h estimate 05-28, 05-29); Maulud declared 08-25 (p-h estimate 08-26).\n- 2016 Fitr: release slug says 5-6 July, body says 6-7 July (amended); p-h 07-06, 07-07 agrees with the body.\n- p-h 'Democracy Day' observed rules and 'Presidential Inauguration Day' 1999+4k agree in outline.\n\nGaps: no official 2015 release (interior.gov.ng 2015 not archived); 2018 Easter, 2019 Oct 1, 2023 Christmas releases not found\n(weekday statutory days kept); Official Gazette notices of the declarations do not appear to be published online; the 2000 and\n2019 amending Acts' gazette texts not found. interior.gov.ng itself returns 503 today; all its pages were read from the Wayback Machine.\n"


